/tmp/tmp.3f5cdLiyZn/introduction.tex:10:\subsection{Problem Definitions}
/tmp/tmp.3f5cdLiyZn/introduction.tex:33:\subsection{Local Search}
/tmp/tmp.3f5cdLiyZn/introduction.tex:41:Theoretical aspects of local search are captured in the definition of the complexity class PLS. 
/tmp/tmp.3f5cdLiyZn/introduction.tex:42:There is a special type of reduction, called PLS-reduction, with respect to which PLS has complete problems \cite{johnson88}.
/tmp/tmp.3f5cdLiyZn/introduction.tex:43:Notably, there are PLS-complete problems, which exhibit two important properties.
/tmp/tmp.3f5cdLiyZn/introduction.tex:44:First, given an instance and an initial solution, it is PSPACE-complete to find a locally optimal solution computed by a local search started with the given initialization.
/tmp/tmp.3f5cdLiyZn/introduction.tex:45:Second, there is an instance and an initial solution, such that this initial solution is exponentially many local search steps away from every locally optimal solution \cite{monien10}.
/tmp/tmp.3f5cdLiyZn/introduction.tex:46:There is a stronger version of PLS-reductions, so-called \emph{tight} PLS-reductions which are of special interest, as they preserve both of these properties \cite{papadimitriou90}.
/tmp/tmp.3f5cdLiyZn/introduction.tex:47:PLS-complete problems having these two properties are therefore sometimes called \emph{tightly} PLS-complete.
/tmp/tmp.3f5cdLiyZn/introduction.tex:57:By \MUFLS and \KMS we denote the respective problem as a PLS-problem associated with the described single-swap neighbourhood.
/tmp/tmp.3f5cdLiyZn/introduction.tex:59:\subsection{Related Work}
/tmp/tmp.3f5cdLiyZn/introduction.tex:78:\subsection{Our Contribution}
/tmp/tmp.3f5cdLiyZn/introduction.tex:80:In this paper, we analyze the PLS complexity of \MUFLS and \KMS.
/tmp/tmp.3f5cdLiyZn/introduction.tex:81:By presenting a tight reduction from \SAT, we show that both problems are tightly PLS-complete, hence that both local search algorithms require exponentially many steps in the worst case and that given some initial solution it is PSPACE-complete to find the solution computed by the respective algorithm started on this initial set of open facilities.
